The code statements that lead to the bug are:
<text>
	if (!priv->tx_skbs)
		return -ENOMEM;
	if (!priv->rxdes)
		return -ENOMEM;
	if (!priv->txdes)
		return -ENOMEM;
</text>
Reason:  
When an allocation for `tx_skbs`, `rxdes`, or `txdes` fails, the function returns `-ENOMEM` directly. However, earlier allocations (e.g., `rx_skbs`, then `tx_skbs`, then `rxdes`) have already succeeded and owned memory that is never freed. This leaks all resources allocated before the failure point.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
<text>
	if (!priv->tx_skbs)
		goto err_free_rx_skbs;
	if (!priv->rxdes)
		goto err_free_tx_skbs;
	if (!priv->txdes)
		goto err_free_rxdes;
...
err_free_rx_skbs:
	kfree(priv->rx_skbs);
	priv->rx_skbs = NULL;
	return -ENOMEM;
</text>
Reason:  
Instead of returning immediately, each failed allocation jumps to a label that frees previously allocated resources in reverse order (e.g., `err_free_tx_skbs` frees `tx_skbs`, then falls through to `err_free_rx_skbs` to free `rx_skbs`). The cleanup also nullifies the pointers to avoid dangling references. This ensures all resources are properly released before returning `-ENOMEM`, eliminating the memory leaks.

